      "writtenOn": "2024-08-20T01:51:38Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "It is not that it is obsolete, it is that it was a hack for lack of better tools for the purpose it was being used for in the first place: preventing HTML, if rendered, from resulting in XSS, by instead causing the HTML to get downloaded.\n\n*That* can be prevented by two means, both of which we employ (the second of which I added after trying to contemplate if there was any way this could ever cause security bugs and deciding to apply another mitigation):\n- Disabling MIME type sniffing so the only HTML that gets loaded is deliberate on the part of the server (which could not be disabled in IE6)\n- Content-Security-Policy preventing loading any scripts even if we *do* serve something as text/html.\n\nMDN does not mention it having a security use case here, for example: https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Headers/Content-Disposition.\n\nLooking around online, it *does* seem like it was intended for security at one point, but we have better tools, and nobody else does this (e.g. api.github.com does not).",
